A||||10||Coupe'N||Duke people...  a lil help here please!||||5||
Z||000000||Coupe'N||06-16-2001||01:26 AM||||Hey everyone, I have a 1987 coupe that has been sitting for at least 6 or 7 months. Before I put the duke to rest, it had a knock. I stopped driving it long distances and only drove it a mile a day to a friends house (who drove me to work). The last day I drove it (100 miles after the knock started) it quit on the way home. I towed it to my house, and was forced into buying a reliable vehicle.<BR>So I go out and reminis with my fiero, and take a look at the engine. I take the valve cover off, and see that all but 2 rockers are loose, the rods are loose as well. Would this have been the demise of my engine??? There are no oil leaks and everything seems to be fine on the outside of the engine. Could the knock have been from the top of the engine? If I was to find a dressed head, and purchase a set of rods, what are the odds of it starting again? Will the duke live and drive again??<BR>ANY input from you guys/gals would be greatly appreciated!<BR>Thanks for the space.<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||danny boy||06-16-2001||02:37 AM||||That may have something to do with it. When I had my 4cyl car I eended up braking a rocker stud and pushrod guideplate twice. Kinda sounded like a bad bearing knock but not exactly. <BR>Hows your oil pressure?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Galen||06-16-2001||03:47 AM||||from what I've heard, dukes are tough engines.. put a lil money in and lets see  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> the least that can happen is it not work<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">     <BR> <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>  -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dwayne Mohr||06-16-2001||08:33 AM||||I just went thru a knock problem with my duke. I was 1 day away from hauling to the Fiero Factory for a new engine ($2000).  It sounded like a diesel when it ran but ran strong and idled smooth. It had me seriously confused. I replaced the lifters thinking the were bad and they were but this did not cure the problem. By the way, depending on where the rods are in the cycle some will be tight and others loose like you described.  <BR>  Anyway, I gave it one more shot with a local speed shop mechanic. He thought it was bad timing gears but when he started it turned out to be the crankshaft bolt on the main pulley had wallered out (his technical jargon). It runs almost perfect again and much better than when I bought it.  I found many vacuum leaks and repaired them while replacing the lifters. Even better the guy only charged $40, I saved $1960 for now. I will probably still do the engine swap later. But for now I am very happy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||06-16-2001||12:46 PM||||If the engine sat without running for 6 months, maybe the hydraulic lifters leaked down.  That would cause loose rockers and pushrods.  If so, no real problem.<P>But that wouldn't explain your knock.  For sure, check for broken timing gears.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-16-2001||12:49 PM||||Depends just what you mean by it quit....<P>87-88 Fiero's are known for knocking under certain conditions. Was it knocking constantly or only at certain times under low speeds and light loads?<P>The valve pushrods are loose or the piston rods are loose? Loose piston rods will likely mean a complete rebuild. Cheaper to find another one in the scrap yards and replace it if that's the case. You can get this motor from Olds Cutlass Calaise and Grand Am's. Cheap.... $200-300 range. maybe less.<P>Why don't you try snugging the rockers and see what happens? That alone could have strangled the engine until it quit. The rockers have stop nuts. Unless the head has been altered you can't over tighten the nuts and make the lifters unhappy.<P>check all the vac lines and gunk like that as well. Don't forget to shoot some oil into the cylinders and crank it over a couple times with a large wrench on the crank bolt. That will help keep you from scoring up the pistons and cylinders. If you can't spin it with a wrench then the starter will likely break something...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Coupe'N||06-16-2001||01:22 PM||||Thanks for your quick replys guys. To answer some of your questions... the knock was there all the time, I took the valve cover off right after it broke down, and noticed that the rockers were loose. I could actually move the rocker arm and pull out a push rod. I then went out yesterday and looked at it again, and they are loose as I stated above. <BR>In that last 100 miles or so, the engine would crank and turn over, but after it warmed up and I came to a stop (idle) it would stall. It wouls still crank, but sometimes it wouldn't turn over. It would have to sit for bit before I could get it to run again. The last time it "stalled/quit" on me, I couldn't get it to turn over at all. This is why I thought I needed a new engine.<BR>Looking at it last night, I was thinking... Maybe if I buy a new head (fierostore.com has them for just under $300) a nice set of push rods, and a new set of rocker arms It may get running again. IF that is the problem.<BR>What are your suggestions on my theory?<BR>Thanks again for your input! AND quick replies!!<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||06-16-2001||01:28 PM||||Try what I said above.... Loose rockers can keep the engine from starting... It's worth a try. Loose rockers will also screw up how the engine runs when it does run.<P>Also,,, If you have an automatic. there is an electrical connector in the fron side of the transmission.... unplug it. that will keep the torque converter from locking. The TC lock could be part of your problem with dieing when you are stopping.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Coupe'N||06-16-2001||05:22 PM||||Thanks OGRE. I did a TCC check when it was running. I remember you saying something like "while driving, touch the brake to see if the idle changes" or something like that. It was working back then, we'll see if it still is working or not.<BR>I am going to do a fluid change (all fluids) before I even try cranking it again. I think it would be wise!<BR>Thanks for your inputs guys.. I'll keep you up to date as I progress!<P>Coupe'N||||reg||12
Z||000009||theogre||06-16-2001||09:14 PM||||change the oil. Check the coolant and make sure it's full... <P>Leave everything else alone unless/until it runs. Not worth messing with the tranny or coolant change until then.<P>BTW... low coolant will also cause knock/ping. It makes the head run hot.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Coupe'N||06-16-2001||10:34 PM||||Change oil, check coolant... Thanks Ogre! Gotcha! Won't mess with the tranny/coolant fluids, just check em'.<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||1
